.TH NETPIPE 1 "June 2026" "netpipe v0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
netpipe \- modular network traffic stream-processing engine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B netpipe
[
.I OPTIONS
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B netpipe
is a high-performance command-line network packet processing tool that operates as a composable, modular stream processor. It captures packets from network interfaces or reads them from files, applies filters and processors, and routes them to one or more output sinks.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Input Options
.TP
.BI \-i " device"
Perform live capture on the specified network interface (e.g., eth0, wlo1, any).
Requires root privileges or the CAP_NET_RAW capability.
.TP
.BI \-r " file.pcap"
Read packets from the specified PCAP file. Does not require root privileges.
.TP
.B \-D
List all available capture devices and exit.
.TP
.BI \-s " snaplen"
Set the maximum bytes captured per packet. Default is 65535 (full packet).
.TP
.B \-p
Disable promiscuous mode. By default, live captures put the network card into promiscuous mode.
.TP
.BI \-T " ms"
Set the read timeout for live captures in milliseconds. Default is 1000 ms.
.SS Filtering Options
Multiple filters are logically ANDed.
.TP
.BI \-f " bpf-expr"
Specify a Berkeley Packet Filter (BPF) expression (e.g., "tcp port 443", "icmp").
.TP
.BI \-proto " name"
Match packets containing the named protocol in their layer stack. Supported names:
.I eth, arp, ip, ip6, icmp, tcp, udp, dns, http, tls.
.TP
.BI \-port " number"
Match packets whose source or destination port equals
.IR number .
.TP
.BI \-host " ip"
Match packets whose source or destination IPv4 address equals
.IR ip .
.SS Output Options
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Write packet data to
.IR file .
If the output format is not specified with
.B \-fmt\fR,
the format is inferred from the file extension (.pcap, .json, .hex, .stats). Use "-" or omit to write to standard output.
.TP
.BI \-fmt " format"
Force the output format. Overrides extension inference. Supported formats:
.RS
.IP "pcap" 8
Wireshark-compatible binary PCAP.
.IP "json" 8
Newline-delimited JSON (NDJSON), one object per packet.
.IP "hex" 8
Human-readable annotated hex dump with layer boundaries.
.IP "stats" 8
Periodic summary of counters.
.IP "null" 8
Discard packets (useful with rate-limiters or stats).
.RE
.TP
.BI \-stats " file"
Write a periodic statistics report to
.I file
(use "-" for stdout) every 5 seconds.
.TP
.BI \-c " count"
Terminate the pipeline after processing
.I count
packets.
.SS Logging Options
.TP
.B \-v
Enable verbose debug logs.
.TP
.B \-vv
Enable extremely verbose trace logs (very noisy).
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet mode. Only warnings and errors are logged.
.TP
.B \-no-color
Disable ANSI color escape sequences in logs.
.SS Miscellaneous Options
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print help information and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.SH RECIPES
.TP
Live capture to PCAP:
.B sudo netpipe \-i eth0 \-o capture.pcap
.TP
Watch HTTP traffic in hex dump:
.B sudo netpipe \-i wlo1 \-f "tcp port 80" \-fmt hex
.TP
Extract DNS logs as JSON:
.B sudo netpipe \-i eth0 \-proto dns \-fmt json
.TP
Analyze PCAP file offline:
.B netpipe \-r capture.pcap \-host 1.2.3.4 \-fmt hex | less
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libnetpipe (3)
